## Why it matters

Imagine a ship with all its compartments open: if one hull breach floods the engine room, the whole ship goes down. In distributed systems, a slow downstream service, a noisy tenant, or a bug can similarly consume shared resources (threads, connections, CPU) and cascade failures across services. The *bulkhead pattern* partitions capacity so faults stay local and the rest of the system keeps sailing.

### Analogy: Watertight compartments

- Ship = your application platform
- Compartments (bulkheads) = resource partitions (thread pools, connection pools, containers)
- Hull breach = a failing or resource-hungry operation

When one compartment floods, the ship still floats because other compartments remain sealed.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  Client[Incoming Requests] --> LoadBalancer
  LoadBalancer --> A[Bulkhead A\n(rate-limited, 10 workers)]
  LoadBalancer --> B[Bulkhead B\n(10 workers)]
  LoadBalancer --> C[Bulkhead C\n(10 workers)]
  A --> |calls| ServiceA
  B --> |calls| ServiceB
  C --> |calls| ServiceC
  ServiceB -->|slow| DB[Slow DB]
  subgraph Failure
    DB
  end
  style Failure fill:#fee,stroke:#f66
```

### How it works (sequence)

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  Client->>API: Request for feature X
  API->>BulkheadX: acquire slot
  BulkheadX-->>API: granted
  API->>Downstream: call
  Downstream-->>API: slow response / timeout
  API->>BulkheadX: release slot
  API-->>Client: fast failure or degrade
```

### Where you apply bulkheads

- **Concurrency limits** per operation (thread pools, async semaphores)
- **Per-tenant** resource quotas in multi-tenant systems
- **Per-service** connection pools (DB, external APIs)
- **Container/Pod** resource limits in orchestrators (CPU, memory)

### Before / After (comparison)

| Situation | Without Bulkheads | With Bulkheads |
|----------|-------------------|----------------|
| Overloaded external API | Entire process thread pool fills, many in-flight requests stall, app becomes unresponsive | Only the compartment serving that integration fills; other operations continue normally |
| Noisy tenant | Noisy tenant consumes DB connections and starves others | Noisy tenant uses its own limited pool; other tenants stay healthy |
| Recovery cost | System-wide restart or long cooldown | Localized backpressure, degraded functionality, faster recovery |

### Practical examples

1) Lightweight JS async bulkhead (semaphore) for outbound HTTP calls

```javascript
// simple async semaphore bulkhead
function createBulkhead(limit){
  let active = 0;
  const queue = [];
  return async function run(fn, timeoutMs = 2000){
    if(active >= limit) {
      throw new Error('BulkheadRejected');
    }
    active++;
    try {
      const result = await Promise.race([
        fn(),
        new Promise((_, rej) => setTimeout(() => rej(new Error('Timeout')), timeoutMs))
      ]);
      return result;
    } finally {
      active--;
      if(queue.length) queue.shift()();
    }
  };
}

// usage
const bulkhead = createBulkhead(10); // max 10 concurrent calls

async function callExternalApi(){
  return bulkhead(async () => {
    // perform fetch/axios call here
  });
}
```

2) Resilience4j (Java) bulkhead usage (thread-pool bulkhead)

```java
// using resilience4j
ThreadPoolBulkheadConfig config = ThreadPoolBulkheadConfig.custom()
  .maxThreadPoolSize(20)
  .coreThreadPoolSize(10)
  .queueCapacity(50)
  .build();
ThreadPoolBulkhead bulkhead = ThreadPoolBulkhead.of("myService", config);

Supplier<CompletionStage<String>> decorated = ThreadPoolBulkhead
  .decorateCompletionStage(bulkhead, () -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> callExternal()));
```

3) Kubernetes-level bulkheads

- Run different critical workloads in separate Deployments/Namespaces with CPU/memory requests & limits.
- Use ResourceQuota and LimitRange to isolate noisy workloads.

### When to use it

- You have multiple independent features, tenants, or downstream integrations that can starve shared resources.
- You need to ensure graceful degradation rather than full outage.
- You want fast, predictable failure modes (fail-fast) for overloaded subsystems.

### Common pitfalls

- **Too-tight limits**: set limits so normal traffic can pass; overly restrictive bulkheads cause unnecessary rejections.
- **Ignoring latency vs concurrency**: bulkheads cap concurrency, not latency; if operations take long, you may still see high queueing.
- **Unpaired timeouts**: if you limit concurrency but don’t set timeouts, workers can still be tied up indefinitely.
- **Hidden shared resources**: bulkheads at the app level won’t protect against shared OS resources like CPU or network saturation.
- **Wrong granularity**: overly coarse bulkheads (entire app) defeat the purpose; overly fine bulkheads add complexity.

### Troubleshooting and metrics

Track metrics per bulkhead: active-concurrency, queue-length, rejected-count, timeouts. Use dashboards and alerts for rising rejections or saturated pools.

### Quick checklist to add bulkheads

- Identify hot paths and shared resources
- Choose granularity (per-integration, per-tenant, per-feature)
- Implement concurrency limits + timeouts + appropriate fallbacks (circuit breakers, degraded response)
- Observe and iterate on limits using production telemetry

### TL;DR

Bulkheads compartmentalize capacity so failures are localized and the system can continue providing partial service. Combine them with timeouts and circuit breakers for robust, graceful degradation.
